{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "label_pos",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "writing_script_complaint",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "Captures a category of audience complaint (weak screenplay, bad dialogue, etc.). The query explicitly targets addressing 'the most common audience complaints', so this is the intervenable lever -- a filmmaker could revise the script. It is the treatment implied by the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "complaint_severity",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Severity is a downstream intensity of the complaint itself, on the pathway from complaint to sentiment, not a common cause of both treatment and outcome. It functions as a mediator/dose measure rather than a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "genre_expectation_mismatch",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Genre expectation mismatch can drive both whether audiences complain (about writing) and their sentiment, making it a plausible common cause. It was not named in the query, so it demonstrates the capability under test -- though genre is arguably closer to an outcome-affecting attribute than a clean confounder of the writing lever."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["writing_script_complaint"],
  "treatment_intervenable": true,
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "genre_expectation_mismatch", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.4}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.3,
  "rationale": "The added columns identify an intervenable treatment: the presence/type of writing-script complaint is something a filmmaker could actually change by revising the script, and the query's 'address the most common complaints' frames exactly this intervention. However, the confounder structure is weak. complaint_severity is a mediator/dose of the complaint, not a common cause of both treatment and outcome, so it does not adjust for confounding. genre_expectation_mismatch is the only column plausibly acting as a common cause of both complaining and sentiment, and it was not named in the query -- but its status is ambiguous (it may itself be an alternative treatment/complaint dimension rather than a clean confounder). No clearly unnamed confounders such as production budget, reviewer disposition, or film reputation are captured. Thus the treatment is intervenable and present, but the interventional identification is under-supported by the confounder set."
}
